===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|ang|sceot}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*skutą}}; compare the {{glossary|doublet}} {{dbt|en|scot|notext=1}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj}}

# Tired, [[weary]].
#: {{syn|en|exhausted|fried|Thesaurus:fatigued}}
#: {{ux|en|I have to go to bed now; I’m '''shot'''.}}
# [[discharged|Discharged]], cleared, or rid ''of'' something.
#: {{syn|en|free}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Tales of My Landlord 3|volume=III|chapter=V|page=62|passage=Tell me true, are you not glad to be fairly '''shot''' of him?}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} [[worn out|Worn out]] or [[broken]].
#: {{syn|en|dilapidated|wrecked|Thesaurus:deteriorated}}
#: {{ux|en|The rear axle will have to be replaced. It’s '''shot'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Help: The Original Human Dilemma|page=50|author=Garret Keizer|year=2004|passage=... but he finds it hard to resist helping the boss's sister, who also works there and whose body "is more '''shot''' than mine."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1998|author=w:The Tragically Hip|chapter=Thompson Girl|title={{w|Phantom Power (The Tragically Hip album)|Phantom Power}}
|passage=Thompson girl, I'm stranded at the Unique Motel / Thompson girl, winterfighter's '''shot''' on the car as well}}
# {{lb|en|of material, especially silk}} Woven from [[warp]] and [[weft]] strands of different colours, resulting in an [[iridescent]] appearance.
#: {{ux|en|The cloak was '''shot''' through with silver threads.}}

